    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with three primary engines powering the offense. **James Wood** (70.34) is our premier play, benefiting from a leadoff spot and a favorable matchup against Alcantara. His 0.377 xwOBA paired with the opponent's slow delivery makes him a prime target for stolen bases. Similarly, **Corbin Carroll** (69.70) remains a core start; while his score is slightly suppressed by a tough catcher and hold-heavy pitcher, his ability to manufacture speed-based value is paramount to our 5x5 strategy. **Trevor Story** (71.87) anchors the middle infield today, as a combination of wind out, favorable park factors, and an advantageous matchup against Martinez push his efficiency well into the elite tier, making him our strongest play by raw score.\n\nPlatoon advantages are driving several middle-tier starts, most notably **Vinnie Pasquantino** (64.21). His 14% boost from a dynamic platoon edge against Hanifee makes him a must-start despite an otherwise neutral environment. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.56) is a high-upside play, buoyed significantly by a massive 16% park adjustment that helps mask his platoon disadvantage against Akin. On the bench, the decision to sit **Cole Young** (42.70) was the most difficult. He hovered near the starting threshold, but his spot at the bottom of the order and a slight penalty for the opponent\u2019s high-efficiency holding of base runners ultimately relegated him behind the higher-volume offensive profiles of **Josh Naylor** (68.37) and **Trevor Story**.\n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Seiya Suzuki** (44.29). While he clears the minimum floor, facing deGrom is a major risk factor that suppresses his SP Skill projection by 19%; he remains in the lineup only because of his spot in the 3-hole and our depth requirements. Additionally, **Brice Turang** (54.75) faces a challenge against Rod\u00f3n, and while his order position helps, owners should monitor the game for any late-inning defensive substitutions. We are keeping **Jose Caballero** (39.32) sidelined, as his score dipped below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor\" due to an unfavorable matchup against Henderson and a significant platoon penalty. **Kyle Karros** (40.48) and **Travis Bazzana** (42.06) were considered, but their overall efficiency marks didn't justify displacing the core starters today.",
